//? Title: NumPy Weights: Typed NPZ Parameters
//? Subtitle: Names and array shapes form a model contract beyond the file format
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Exported numeric arrays become model weights only when their names and shapes match a known
computation. We load native NumPy-format affine weights into a native store, compare stored and
compressed NPZ, and preserve scalar, empty and large-integer tensors. The examples separate
valid numeric storage from a valid serving model.
)
\toc

> The method

For input X with two columns, weights W of shape [2,1] and bias b of shape [1], the serving
contract is Y=XW+b. NPZ member names identify W and b; array metadata establishes dtypes and
shapes. ParameterStore contains floating learnable values, so generic state such as integer
counters belongs in NamedTensors instead. With W=(.75,-1.5) and b=.25,

$$
f(2,-3)=2(3/4)-3(-3/2)+1/4=25/4.
$$
\alttext(The two-feature affine prediction is six point two five.)

Stored and deflate-compressed archives represent the same arrays. Bool, Int32, Int64, Float32
and Float64 are native interchange types; narrow integer arrays widen on read. A scalar shape
is different from [1], and a zero-sized axis must retain its dimensions. Native writers produce
C-order little-endian NPY 1.0; the decoder also accepts supported newer header versions. CRC and
length checks reject damaged data. No Python pickle or graph code is executed.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/numpy_weights_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/numpy_weights_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/numpy_weights_concept.tikz > build/numpy_weights_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/numpy_weights_concept.svg build/numpy_weights_concept.dvi > build/numpy_weights_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/numpy_weights_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/numpy_weights_concept.svg from local TikZ source.
// \image(figures/numpy_weights_concept.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Names and array shapes form a model contract beyond the file format)
\alttext(NPZ named arrays; weights [2,1], bias [1] Then Validate dtype + shape; known affine function Then Native prediction; Y=XW+b.)

> Simple examples

```{cpp, results="output", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/nn/array_io.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const n::NamedTensors arrays{{"weights",n::tensor({.75,-1.5},{2,1}).to(n::DType::Float32)},{"bias",n::tensor({.25}).to(n::DType::Float32)}};
 n::save_npz("build/numpy_weights_stored.npz",arrays);
 n::save_npz("build/numpy_weights_deflated.npz",arrays,true);
 std::print("Native C++ wrote the same two named arrays in stored and deflated archives.\n");
}
```
// result_begin:
// Native C++ wrote the same two named arrays in stored and deflated archives.
// result_end

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/numpy_weights_prediction.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;n::ParameterStore s;s.load("build/numpy_weights_stored.npz",false);
 const auto stored=n::load_npz("build/numpy_weights_stored.npz"),compressed=n::load_npz("build/numpy_weights_deflated.npz");
 for(size_t i=0;i<stored.size();++i)if(n::encode_npy(stored[i].second)!=n::encode_npy(compressed[i].second))return 1;
 const auto x=n::tensor({{0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{2,-3}}),y=n::affine_inference(x,s.at("weights"),s.at("bias"));
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.bars({0,1,2,3},y.to_vector<double>(),"prediction",{37,99,235},.5)
 .title("The weights are interpreted by a serving function").x_label("query row").y_label("prediction");
 p.size(820,420).save_svg("build/numpy_weights_prediction.svg");
 if(y.at({3,0})!=6.25)return 1;
 std::print("Predictions: {}.\n\nStored and compressed members have identical native NPY encodings.\n",y.to_vector<double>());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Predictions: [0.25, 1, -1.25, 6.25].

Stored and compressed members have identical native NPY encodings.
\image(figures/numpy_weights_prediction.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Known weights produce .25, 1, -1.25 and 6.25 for the four query rows.)
\alttext(Known weights produce .25, 1, -1.25 and 6.25 for the four query rows.)

> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 const n::NamedTensors arrays{{"counter",n::from_vector(std::vector<int64_t>{(int64_t{1}<<40)+7},{}).reshape({})},{"empty",n::zeros({2,0,3})},{"bool",n::tensor({0,1},{2},n::DType::Bool)}};
 const auto decoded=n::decode_npz(n::encode_npz(arrays));
 for(size_t i=0;i<arrays.size();++i)if(n::encode_npy(arrays[i].second)!=n::encode_npy(decoded[i].second))return 1;
 auto bytes=n::encode_npy(n::zeros({2,3}));bytes.pop_back();int bad=0;try{n::decode_npy(bytes);}catch(const std::exception&){++bad;}
 if(bad!=1)return 1;
 std::print("Int64 scalar: {}; empty shape: {}; Bool values: {}.\n\nTruncated NPY rejected.\n",decoded[0].second.item<int64_t>(),n::shape_str(decoded[1].second.shape()),decoded[2].second.to_vector<int>());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Int64 scalar: 1099511627783; empty shape: [2, 0, 3]; Bool values: [0, 1].

Truncated NPY rejected.
// result_end


> Conclusion

File encodings preserve dtype and shape, while the affine contract gives the arrays predictive
meaning. Generic integer and boolean state remains outside ParameterStore. For explicit byte-order and layout checks, see the companion numpy_interop report. Independent NumPy-written arrays are retained in the native regression tests.

> References

- [NumPy NPY format specification](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.lib.format.html).
